     "size": 28,
     "color": "#C00000"
    }
   ],
   "speech": "What if we optimize several images at once, and they have to share pixels? Look at the giraffe. Turn it upside down, and it's a penguin. This dog, upside down, is an elephant. No model is fine-tuned. It's Peekaboo's recipe, a frozen prior plus score distillation, over a new parameterization: Stable Diffusion, and SDXL for the numbers."
  },
  {
   "title": "One print, two pictures",
   "elements": [
    {
     "kind": "image",
     "path": "/Users/rburgert/Documents/ThesisProposal/presentation/agents/.scratchpad/explain_diffillusions/crop_flip_photo.png",
     "rect": [
      100,
      100,
      760,
      401.9
     ]
    }
   ],
   "speech": "Here's one we printed. Right side up, a dog. Rotate the paper, a sloth. The red words are just handwritten labels. The paper is the prime image, the thing you hold. Each way of looking at it is an arrangement, a fixed function that's differentiable and physically doable. What you see is a derived image, and each one gets its own prompt. One prime, two arrangements, identity and rotate 180."
  },
  {
   "title": "But...how?",
   "elements": [
    {
     "kind": "image",
     "path": "/Users/rburgert/Documents/ThesisProposal/presentation/agents/.scratchpad/explain_diffillusions/arch.png",
     "rect": [
      30,
      92,
      900,
      372.6
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "text",
     "text": "Every view gets a loss; all gradients land on the shared primes.",
     "rect": [
      40,
      480,
      880,
      40
     ],
     "size": 24,
     "color": "#C00000"
    }
   ],
   "speech": "But...how? Here's the whole pipeline. Each prime is a small Fourier feature network, an MLP from pixel coordinates to color. The arrangements turn primes into derived images. On each derived image we run score distillation with its prompt: encode, add noise, the frozen model predicts the noise, and the error is a gradient on that view. No backprop through the U-Net, only through the encoder, the arrangements and the MLPs, so every view's gradient sums onto the shared primes. Why an MLP? Fourier features plus a small MLP make a smooth image, so the second picture can't hide in pixel noise that printing would wipe out."
  },
