    {
     "kind": "text",
     "text": "Light through two sheets = multiply the colors",
     "rect": [
      290,
      400,
      660,
      34
     ],
     "size": 24,
     "color": "#C00000",
     "align": "center"
    },
    {
     "kind": "text",
     "text": "d_j = tanh(2 · p1 · rot(p2, 90°·j)),  j = 0..3",
     "rect": [
      290,
      442,
      660,
      30
     ],
     "size": 20,
     "color": "#000000",
     "align": "center"
    }
   ],
   "speech": "What else can an arrangement be? Print two images on transparencies over a backlight. The base stays, the rotator spins on top. Light goes through both, so the color you see is the product. Order doesn't matter, multiplication commutes. Products get dark, so we multiply by two, a brighter backlight, and a tanh keeps it in range. Two primes, four rotations, four derived images: cat, man, woman, dog. Simulated here; the paper has photos of the prints."
  },
